### The standards

The documents whose codes the slot table of `{{ROOT}}/README.md` names, with `SDD-01` above
them. Each is generated from a script and none may be edited by hand. Their folder's
`README.md` is the index; its `CROSSWALK_old_names_to_SDD.md`
resolves any citation written before 31 August 2026 — **four `SDD-` codes changed meaning
that evening**, so check any code you inherit.

**Read before you plan:** `SDD-01` §§4, 5, 9, 10, 12, 14, 16 and 17; the standards
`README.md`; and the standards folder's open-findings register. **Which of them are in
force and which are drafts is read from their own first pages, not from this commission.**

**Each slot's checklist and template are produced by its standard's build**, and stand in the
delivery kit under `templates/spec/checklists/` and `templates/spec/artefacts/`, named in each
slot's row of `templates/spec/slots.yaml` — the checklist carrying the gate, the form's lines and
the rules, the template the form's lines to fill. `kit slot <n>` prints both for a slot.

**`SDD-01` §10 names {{HANDOVERS}} handovers**, with what is checked at each and how much of that
checking a machine does, and which rest on a person alone. **Your plan must say where each of
the {{HANDOVERS}} falls in it, and which this
run can actually check.**

**`SDD-01` §14** carries **the standing rules** — count them there; §12 the three tests for when a
specification is good enough — the developer test, the review test, the refusal test — and
the rule about honesty that fails first when a deadline is close.

**The rule population you will claim against.** Every rule-bearing standard's family, counted
**from the standards themselves and not from any index.** Arithmetic over a stated range is
not a count: at least one family in this set carries fewer rules than its range has numbers,
because rules were withdrawn before issue. **Print what you counted, per standard.**

### What is weak in the standards, and must be planned around rather than discovered

Each standard states its own standing and its own evidence position on its first page, and
several say plainly that their rules rest on this organisation's judgement rather than on
published practice. **Read those statements and carry them into the plan**, because a slot
governed by a standard whose rules are positions will produce more amendments than a slot
governed by one whose rules are evidenced, and the plan should expect that rather than be
surprised by it.

**`SDD-01` §17 names things the method requires and no standard governs.** Do not fill them
speculatively and do not plan work that assumes a standard for them will appear. Where the
carried material contains such a thing, **say what happens to it.**

**Where a standard assigns work to a role, check that somebody holds it.** A standard that
assigns a slot to a person who does not exist is a decision for the owner before the slot
opens, not a gap to work around.

