## 4. What the plan must contain

Hold it as **one YAML record**, render it, and never type the page. A correction goes into
the record and the page is rendered again; the renderer's `--check` refuses a page that
differs from what the record renders now.

The record carries, in this order:

1. **`meta`** — version, date, session, this commission's path, **the reader** (a named
   person who has not worked on this system and must start the first piece of work on the
   first morning without asking a question the page could have answered), `read_from`, and
   **`not_used`** — every file opened and deliberately not relied on, with the reason.
2. **`rule_zero`** — measure before you assert; read the artefact, not the document about
   the artefact; where the two disagree the artefact wins and the disagreement is a finding.
3. **`the_words`** — every term that carries weight, defined once. **Where a standard's word
   and this system's existing word mean the same thing, say so in one line.** Across the
   standards expect many.
4. **`what_is_being_built`**, and **`what_this_design_is_not`.**
5. **`what_was_carried_in`** — what each carried artefact holds, measured, with the command
   that measured it; and **what none of it carries**, which is most of what the {{THINGS}} slots
   need.
6. **`the_slot_map`** — the centre of the plan. **One row per slot, {{SLOT_LIST}}**, carrying: the
   governing standard and its rule family (or *nothing governs it*); what the carried
   material gives towards it; the verdict — *have it · half of it · nothing*; whether the
   work is **compile, research or test**; who does it; and what it costs. Slots 08 and 10
   carry a row saying what produces them and from what.
7. **`the_three_problems`** — §3 above, each with the plan's answer to it.
8. **`the_conformance_bar`** — the rule count you counted, per standard, and how a claim is
   recorded rule by rule in `_conformance/`. **A claim that is not rule by rule is not a
   claim; it is an opinion.** Any declared departure of the method from a standard must be
   named as part of the claim against that standard.
9. **`the_amendment_register`** — where and how every piece of work records what the
   standards cost it, **with the moment it happened.** This is what the run is for.
10. **`pieces`** — the work. Each with an id, a name, what it produces **by path inside
    `{{ROOT}}/`**, its kind, its inputs, who does it, one honest sitting estimate, **what it
    answers of the governing standard**, and **what tells the person doing it that they got
    it wrong** — a program where a program can, a named reader where only a person can. A
    piece with nothing that can tell it it is wrong says so, and says why.
11. **`what_forces_that_order`** — the load-bearing links only, argued. Not a restatement of
    the list.
12. **`the_handovers`** — where each of `SDD-01` §10's handovers falls, and which
    this run can actually check.
13. **`the_questions_this_design_does_not_settle`**, **`what_this_run_will_not_do`**,
    **`the_decisions_the_owner_must_make_first`** — each with a recommendation and the case
    against.
14. **`the_figures`**, **`what_cannot_be_counted`**, **`statements_carried_without_counting`**,
    **`attacking_this_design_before_filing_it`**, **`met_and_not_repaired`**,
    **`lines_for_a_register_not_applied`**, **`how_every_figure_was_obtained`**.

**Every figure on the page is obtained by a command that is printed. A count nobody counted
may not appear.**

