## 7. What refuses

- Writing any part of the specification. **You are planning, not filling.**
- Authoring a requirement, a record, a use case, a screen, a relationship name or a type.
- Editing `00_customer_documents/` for any reason whatever.
- Editing a generated document by hand.
- Declaring a carried artefact correct, or resolving §3.1 by assertion.
- Inventing a standard, or planning work that assumes a missing one will appear.
- Acting on your own finding. **A gap is a finding with an owner, never a local invention.**
- Reporting a count you did not count, or a check you did not watch refuse something.
- Softening a check, or adjusting a threshold to clear a refusal.
- Filling a slot folder to make the tree look finished.
- Accepting this commission's facts. **Re-measure them.**

---

## 8. The closing report

Short. The owner does not want a long report of what you did. He wants what is true, what is
decided, what is waiting on him, and what you got wrong.

It carries: **the plan in ten lines**; every figure that overturned something this commission
asserts; the findings you raised with their owners; the decisions for the owner, **each with
a recommendation and none pre-selected**; **what you did not look at**; and **your own
errors, first and in the same breath.**

If the decisions run past three, put them on one clickable page rather than as cards in a
reply — and **do not block on it.** Raise it, say it exists, and carry on with everything the
answer does not gate.

---

## 9. What this commission does not cover

It stops when the plan is filed. Performing the plan — writing the register, drawing the
model, describing the goals, working out the screens — is commissioned separately, one piece
of work at a time, each against the plan this run produces.

